# Muse settings for your own PingOne and PingFederate.
# Copy this file to .env and fill in what you use:  cp .env.example .env
# `npm start` reads .env if it exists. Nothing here is needed for the mock demo: leave .env out and Muse runs fully offline.
# .env is git-ignored. Never commit it, and keep client secrets out of chat and tickets.
# What to create in PingOne first: see README, "Use your own PingOne and PingFederate".

# ---- Real mode (owner-run). Without these two Muse stays mock. ----------------------------------------------
ALLOW_LIVE=1
# The MCP gateway Muse calls with the delegated token (Agent Gateway, or an Agent Privilege app in front of it). Must end in /mcp.
MUSE_LIVE_GATEWAY_URL=https://gateway.example/<app>/mcp
# Extra https origins Muse may contact besides the gateway (comma separated). Usually empty.
MUSE_LIVE_ALLOW_ORIGINS=

# ---- PingOne ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your environment id (PingOne console: Environment > Properties).
MUSE_P1_ENV_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# Region of your tenant: com (North America), eu, ca or asia
MUSE_P1_REGION=com
# Muse's own app (OIDC Web App: authorization code + PKCE, client credentials, token exchange; client secret post).
MUSE_P1_CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# Its secret. Prefer the file form (one line, chmod 600) over pasting it here.
MUSE_P1_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/path/to/muse-client-secret
# MUSE_P1_CLIENT_SECRET=
# gateway: Muse calls Agent Gateway directly. privilege: Muse calls an Agent Privilege app that fronts the gateway.
MUSE_P1_LANE=gateway
# Scopes: Muse's own agent token (client credentials), and the scope the delegated token carries for the gateway.
MUSE_P1_ACTOR_SCOPE=agent:invoke
MUSE_P1_GATEWAY_SCOPE=gateway:mcp:invoke
# The demo customer's username. The DaVinci flow reads suffixes Muse adds (.conn = came through the connector, .nomfa = skip MFA).
MUSE_P1_LOGIN_HINT=demouser
# Optional: a second app with no agent (the "Just a user, no agent" button). Its token has no act claim, so the gateway refuses it.
MUSE_USER_CLIENT_ID=

# ---- Test controls in Book a flight (optional, Real only, opt-in, owner-approved 2026-11-09) -----------------------
# DEVIATION from the standalone rule "no PingOne admin credentials in an app": this one feature may hold a runtime credential, so the
# owner can flip airlineMemberTier between Premier Gold and Premier Silver, and set the agentSpendLimit (whole dollars), from the panel.
# Constraints: an explicit opt-in (MUSE_TIER_ENABLE=1, required even in Docker, where MUSE_HOSTED=1 is always set; the SE deployment never
# sets it), a dedicated worker app with only the permission to update users (never the shared admin worker in provisioning.json), supplied
# here and nowhere else, never committed. Muse writes only airlineMemberTier ("Premier Gold", "Premier Silver" or clear) and agentSpendLimit
# (1 to 100000 as text, or clear), only on the user ids below, in one write per user, and reads each user back to prove it. The secret is
# write-only: never returned, logged or shown. Off unless the opt-in and all of the settings are present.
# The worker app needs client secret basic on its token endpoint. Environment and region default to MUSE_P1_ENV_ID / MUSE_P1_REGION.
# The opt-in flag (name only here; set it to 1 in your own .env to turn the controls on):
# MUSE_TIER_ENABLE=1
# For a PUBLIC hosted URL use read instead: Muse then only READS the demo users (the connector's limit for the pre-approval, the card's Check PingOne)
# and refuses every write with no PingOne call. Exactly 1 writes, exactly read reads, any other value is off. NEVER set it to 1 on a public URL.
# MUSE_TIER_ENABLE=read
# MUSE_TIER_WORKER_CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# MUSE_TIER_WORKER_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Comma separated PingOne user ids the switch may touch (the demo users), at most 5.
# MUSE_TIER_USER_IDS=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000,00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# MUSE_TIER_ENV_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# MUSE_TIER_REGION=com

# ---- Airline Connector (Muse as an MCP server other assistants connect to) -------------------------------
# Needs real mode above. MUSE_PUBLIC_URL is where PingOne and the assistants reach Muse: https, or http on loopback for a local run.
MUSE_CONNECTOR=0
MUSE_PUBLIC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5100
MUSE_CONNECTOR_AIRLINE=SkyHigh
# Callback hosts allowed to register (comma separated), for example chatgpt.com,claude.ai. Muse's own /sim/<platform>/callback always may.
MUSE_CONNECTOR_REDIRECT_HOSTS=
# Optional pre-registered client, for an assistant that asks for a client id instead of registering.
MUSE_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_ID=
MUSE_CONNECTOR_REDIRECT_URIS=
# Optional: an own PingOne app per assistant, so Protect and the sign-in screens name it. JSON on one line:
# [{"name":"ChatGPT","hosts":["chatgpt.com"],"clientId":"<app id>","clientSecret":"<secret>"},{"name":"Claude","hosts":["claude.ai","claude.com"],"clientId":"<app id>","clientSecret":"<secret>"},{"name":"Codex","hosts":[],"clientId":"<app id>","clientSecret":"<secret>"}]
MUSE_CONNECTOR_AGENTS=
# Consent bridge for book_flight (optional, owner-run; exception #2, owner-approved 2026-11-09). The gateway's trusted-caller secret, so the connector can pass on
# the "act for you" consent the user gave at sign-in (headers x-internal-gateway-secret and X-May-Act-Sub, on the book_flight tools/call only). Write-only: never
# returned or logged. Set the value in your own .env only, never in a file you commit. Unset: bookings are denied for missing consent and the message says why.
# With it set, book_flight also sends X-Hitl-Preapproved: user-limit (and X-Agent-Spend-Limit: your limit, read from PingOne through the MUSE_TIER_* test controls above)
# so a booking within your limit skips the gateway's approval hold. Without the MUSE_TIER_* worker credential the gateway's default limit of 500 applies.
# MUSE_GATEWAY_INTERNAL_SECRET=

# ---- PingOne Protect through DaVinci (optional, owner-run) ------------------------------------------------
# PROTECT_MODE=live
# PROTECT_FLOW_URL=https://auth.pingone.com/<env id>/davinci/policy/<policy id>/start
# PROTECT_API_KEY=
# PROTECT_RISK_POLICY_ID=

# ---- PingFederate (the CIMD path, optional, owner-run) -----------------------------------------------------
# ChatGPT, Claude and Codex present a client ID metadata document (CIMD) to a real PingFederate 13.1 or later instead of registering.
# Muse then serves /mcp-pf (PingFederate is its authorization server), the labelled document copies at /sim/<platform>/client.json and
# ChatGPT's JWKS. Needs real mode and an https MUSE_PUBLIC_URL that PingFederate can reach. In PingFederate: CIMD on, one CIMD policy per
# document URL (PKCE required), a JWT access token manager with its own signing key and aud = <MUSE_PUBLIC_URL>/mcp-pf, the scope below,
# and an access token mapping from your sign-in adapter to that token manager. See README, "Use your own PingOne and PingFederate".
# MUSE_PF_ISSUER=https://pf.example:9031
# MUSE_PF_SCOPE=muse:mcp

# ---- Local model (optional) -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An OpenAI-compatible server on this machine, used to pick the tool and word the reply. Loopback only.
# MUSE_LLM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8090
# MUSE_LLM_MODEL=local
